// [undocumented rule; was old-book ch05 §5.14] (Generic Types): `new T(...)`'s "dotted clone"
// constructor-selection rule used to only check whether a candidate's
// mangled name *ended with* ".T_new" -- a monomorphized generic
// instantiation mangles its own type argument into its name, so
// `std::shared_ptr<Payload>`'s own constructor is named
// `std::shared_ptr.Payload_new`, which ends with ".Payload_new" and
// therefore satisfied a plain `new Payload()` looking for `Payload_new`.
// codegen then ran shared_ptr's own (larger) constructor over a
// Payload-sized heap block, smashing malloc's top chunk -- the symptom
// was a `Fatal glibc error: malloc.c:2371 (sysmalloc): assertion failed`
// at some later, unrelated allocation (exit=134 on origin/main before
// the fix, PR #392). The dotted-clone rule now also requires the text
// before that '.' to be the candidate's own owner class, so a foreign
// generic instantiation is never mistaken for one of `Payload`'s own
// constructors. `guard` first forces `std::shared_ptr<Payload>` (and
// therefore its whole constructor set, including its 0-arg default
// constructor) to be monomorphized; the plain `new Payload()` right
// after must still allocate a correctly Payload-sized, Payload-shaped
// object, and the loop of further heap churn afterward is there so any
// reintroduced heap corruption reliably crashes rather than staying
// silently unnoticed.
import std;

struct Payload {
    int value;
};

int main() {
    Payload* guard_ptr{};
    [[scpp::unsafe]] {
        guard_ptr = new Payload();
    }
    std::shared_ptr<Payload> guard{guard_ptr};

    int result = 0;
    [[scpp::unsafe]] {
        Payload* p = new Payload();
        p->value = 7;
        result = p->value;
        delete p;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 64; i = i + 1) {
        [[scpp::unsafe]] {
            int* filler = new int(i);
            delete filler;
        }
    }

    return result - 7;
}
